/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> feetDoubleBassEighthsLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "An even stream of eighths split between two feet is the first double bass skill: one foot plays the beats, the other the \"ands\", and together they sound like one drummer with a very fast kick. Every faster double bass pattern grows out of this, and the variations here teach you to start, stop, group and accent the stream with either foot.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Two pedals (or a double pedal), heel up and loose, both feet at the same height and the same volume. Line 1 is the reference: right foot on every beat, left foot on every \"and\", hat quarters and the backbeat on 2 and 4 above. The R and L under the feet tell you which foot plays.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Line 2 is the same stream with the left foot leading — slow, until the left foot is as steady as the right. Line 3 plays a pair R L on 1 and 3 and rests on 2 and 4 under eighth hats; line 4 starts each pair on the \"and\" so it runs into the beat, with the right hand on the ride. Line 5 groups the eighths 3+3+2 with accents on 1, the \"and\" of 2 and 4, and the hat plays the same accents. Line 6 is half a bar of stream and two right-foot quarters on the ride, line 7 the full stream under a half-time snare on 3 and eighth hats, line 8 a crash with a plain 1 and 3 and then a burst on 3 and 4. Start slow — 60 is fine.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Motörhead \"Overkill\" with Phil Taylor, Judas Priest and Accept in the early-80s heavy metal era, and the steady double-bass choruses of countless metal songs before they speed up to sixteenths.",
  ),
];
